<article class="env-lemma" id="00GR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00GR">Lemma <span data-tag="00GR">10.36.18</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $K$ be a field. If $R \subset K$ and $K$ is integral over $R$, then $R$ is a field and $K$ is an algebraic extension. If $R \subset K$ and $K$ is finite over $R$, then $R$ is a field and $K$ is a finite algebraic extension. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume that $R \subset K$ is integral. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00GQ" data-tag="00GQ">10.36.17</a> we see that $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ has $1$ point. Since clearly $R$ is a domain we see that $R = R_{(0)}$ is a field (Lemma <a href="/tag/00EU" data-tag="00EU">10.25.1</a>). The other assertions are immediate from this. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>